{"excerpt": "[2edd20cc5e66] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Both properties are required, but at different architectural layers. The writer must be safe (retry attempts are harmless or detectably redundant), and the replay/deduplication layer must be idempotent in its effect on derived state. The distinction between idempotent and safe is thus a layer-boundary distinction: safety is the property demanded of the producer (it may append duplicates, but they must be identifiable), while idempotency is the property demanded of the consumer/replay engine (processing a duplicate must not change derived state beyond the first processing). The append-only log requires safety of its writers and idempotency of its readers, not idempotency of its writers.\nMECHANISM: The system decomposes into a write path and a read/replay path. On the write path, at-least-once delivery means the writer may submit the same event mult", "handle": "ART_3", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[932ef2e29b90] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: The log actually requires idempotency of writers, not merely safety, because deterministic replay demands that the replayed state be exactly identical to the state that would have been produced without retries. Safety, which permits state divergence as long as invariants hold, is insufficient for deterministic replay: a safe-but-not-idempotent writer could produce log states [E] under no-retry and [E,E-dup] under retry, where E-dup is a deduplicated stub. Even if downstream consumers handle both correctly, the raw log itself differs, breaking byte-for-byte replay determinism. Only true idempotency—guaranteeing the writer's effect on the log is identical regardless of retry count—satisfies deterministic replay.\nMECHANISM: Deterministic replay requires that the log's contents are a function of the sequence of logical operations, not of delivery retr", "handle": "ART_4", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[2dcb167045a9] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: The distinction between idempotent and safe is a false dichotomy for append-only logs. What the log actually requires of its writers is commutativity with respect to the deduplication function: appending E then E (retry) must yield the same deduplicated result as appending E then E in the opposite order, or appending E once and then the retry. The relevant property is neither idempotency (f(f(x))=f(x)) nor safety (invariants preserved), but rather that the deduplication function D applied to the log is a monotonically increasing fixed point: D(log + [E]) = D(log + [E, E]) = D(D(log + [E]) + [E]). The log requires writers whose operations commute under D.\nMECHANISM: Idempotency is a property of a single operation applied multiple times. Safety is a property of system invariants under retries. But the append-only log's real requirement is about the ", "handle": "ART_5", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[f3cb5758ff25] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: In practice, append-only event logs such as Apache Kafka achieve at-least-once delivery safety through idempotent producers at the protocol level, but this is a misnomer: the producer's 'idempotency' is actually safety achieved via sequence numbers and transactional IDs that allow the broker to deduplicate. The append to the log is never idempotent—the broker still writes entries—it is the producer-broker protocol that is safe. Published definitions from the messaging literature (e.g., Burnstein & Shapiro on exactly-once semantics) distinguish idempotency as an application-level property from safety as a protocol-level property. The log requires safety of the writer-broker protocol, and idempotency is an emergent property of the consumer's state machine, not a requirement on writers.\nMECHANISM: Kafka's idempotent producer assigns a Producer ID (PI", "handle": "ART_6", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[2e61be98723f] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: Idempotent operations satisfy f(f(x))=f(x): repeated application converges to the same state as a single application. Safe operations merely cause no corruption or harm when repeated, but their repeated state may differ from the single-application state. An append-only event log requires only that its writers be safe—retried appends produce duplicate entries but no corruption—while idempotency is deferred to the replay processor, which must deduplicate (via sequence numbers, idempotency keys, or hash-based collapsing) to achieve deterministic replay.\nMECHANISM: An append to the log is inherently non-idempotent: appending the same event twice yields two entries, a different state from appending once. But it is safe: the log tolerates duplicates without corruption, violating no invariant. Deterministic replay survives because the replay layer applie", "handle": "ART_7", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[bbf87d372e3f] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: The published distinction (per RFC 9110 §9.2.1) is sharper than the informal one: 'safe' means the operation has no side effects at all—it is semantically read-only, intended to be callable without any risk of changing observable state. 'Idempotent' means repeated identical invocations have the same effect as a single one, but side effects are permitted. An append-only event log's writers are neither safe nor idempotent in this strict sense. What the log actually requires is a weaker, informal 'retry-safe' property that neither RFC term captures: retried writes may produce duplicate entries, but those duplicates are tolerated by the replay protocol.\nMECHANISM: In RFC 9110 terminology, safe = read-only (GET), idempotent = convergent (PUT, DELETE). An append is POST-like: it has side effects (new entry) and is not convergent (two appends ≠ one). So ", "handle": "ART_8", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[7db46860ddab] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: The append-only log actually requires idempotent writers, not merely safe ones, because deterministic replay demands that the replayed system state match the original state. If retried appends produce duplicate log entries that the original execution did not produce, replay is non-deterministic. Only idempotent writes—achieved through idempotency keys that the log layer deduplicates—ensure that the replayed log is semantically identical to the original.\nMECHANISM: Consider a writer that appends an event without an idempotency key. Under at-least-once delivery, the same event is appended twice. During replay, the processor sees two entries where the original saw one. If the processor is a state reducer (e.g., summing values), the replayed state differs from the original. This violates deterministic replay. The fix is idempotency keys at the write l", "handle": "ART_9", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[d3d4e3525751] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: The distinction between idempotent and safe is best understood through counterexample. A writer that appends a random UUID per call is safe (retries don't corrupt the log) but not idempotent (each retry produces a different entry). A writer that appends a deterministic hash of the payload is both safe and idempotent (same payload always yields the same entry, deduplicatable). A writer that increments a global counter and appends the result is neither safe nor idempotent (retries corrupt the counter and produce divergent entries). The append-only log requires the safe-and-non-idempotent writer at minimum, but prefers the safe-and-idempotent writer for robust deterministic replay.\nMECHANISM: Three archetypes: (a) random UUID writer—safe, non-idempotent; the log has duplicates but replay can filter by content hash if payloads are identical. (b) deter", "handle": "ART_10", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[14e084ed5ca7] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: The distinction is a category error in the context of append-only logs: idempotency and safety are properties of operations, but the append-only log requires a property of the delivery protocol—namely, that retried writes are distinguishable from original writes by the replay layer. This 'distinguishability' is neither idempotency nor safety; it is a protocol-level invariant that the log enforces through metadata (delivery IDs, producer epochs, sequence numbers). The writers need only attach this metadata; they need not themselves be idempotent or safe.\nMECHANISM: The real requirement is that the replay layer can tell which entries are duplicates of which. This is a protocol property (delivery metadata), not an operation property (idempotency/safety). A writer that attaches a unique delivery ID to each attempt is neither idempotent (different IDs ", "handle": "ART_11", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
